A direct in-place version. The left side of the array stays sorted, and each new value is moved left until it reaches the right position.

For example, `[5, 2, 4]` becomes `[2, 5, 4]` after inserting `2`, then `[2, 4, 5]` after inserting `4`. The input array is changed during sorting.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> insertionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            int current = nums[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            while (j >= 0 && nums[j] > current) {
                nums[j + 1] = nums[j];
                j--;
            }

            nums[j + 1] = current;
        }

        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case and `O(n)` when the array is already sorted.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This keeps the usual insertion sort idea, but finds the insertion position with binary search. Once the position is known, the values after it are shifted one place to the right.

A small detail is that binary search reduces comparisons, but shifting can still take linear time. The array is sorted in place.

Dry run for `[4, 1, 3]`:
- Insert `1` before `4`, giving `[1, 4, 3]`.
- Insert `3` between `1` and `4`, giving `[1, 3, 4]`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] insertionSort(int[] nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int value = nums[i];
            int left = 0;
            int right = i;

            while (left < right) {
                int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
                if (nums[middle] <= value) {
                    left = middle + 1;
                } else {
                    right = middle;
                }
            }

            for (int j = i; j > left; j--) {
                nums[j] = nums[j - 1];
            }
            nums[left] = value;
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because shifting values can take `O(n)` for each item. The comparisons take `O(log n)` per item.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Here the array is split into smaller parts and then merged back in sorted order. The merge step always takes the smaller front value from the two sorted parts.

This is different from moving items one at a time. It also needs a temporary vector for merging, and the original array is updated with the final sorted values.

For `[6, 2, 5, 1]`, the smaller pieces are sorted first, then merged as `[2, 6]` and `[1, 5]`, which finally gives `[1, 2, 5, 6]`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> insertionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> temporary(nums.size());
        mergeSort(nums, temporary, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        return nums;
    }

private:
    void mergeSort(vector<int>& nums, vector<int>& temporary, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        mergeSort(nums, temporary, left, middle);
        mergeSort(nums, temporary, middle + 1, right);

        int i = left;
        int j = middle + 1;
        int position = left;

        while (i <= middle && j <= right) {
            if (nums[i] <= nums[j]) {
                temporary[position++] = nums[i++];
            } else {
                temporary[position++] = nums[j++];
            }
        }

        while (i <= middle) {
            temporary[position++] = nums[i++];
        }

        while (j <= right) {
            temporary[position++] = nums[j++];
        }

        for (int index = left; index <= right; index++) {
            nums[index] = temporary[index];
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for splitting and merging the array.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the temporary vector and recursion calls.

This is a swap-based version of insertion sorting. Whenever a value is smaller than the value before it, adjacent elements are swapped until the value reaches its place.

It is easy to follow because every swap fixes one local inversion. If the array is already sorted, no swaps are made.

One edge case is a list with repeated values. The strict `>` check keeps equal values from being swapped unnecessarily.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] insertionSort(int[] nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int current = i;

            while (current > 0 && nums[current] < nums[current - 1]) {
                int temporary = nums[current];
                nums[current] = nums[current - 1];
                nums[current - 1] = temporary;
                current--;
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case, such as when the values are in descending order.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

